(100125,'AIE6008','计算神经科学','','AIE - Artificial Intelligence','6008','A',3.00,6000,'Major','已评分','讲演','Main Campus','人工智能学院','人工智能学院','saved_outline','saved_terms','2026-27 Term 1','This course is designed for master students and assumes foundational knowledge from prior undergraduate’s level study. Specifically:

1) Artificial Intelligence / Machine Learning: Familiarity with basic concepts such as neural networks, supervised/unsupervised learning, and backpropagation (equivalent to an introductory machine learning course at the undergraduate or master''s level).
2) Mathematics: Working knowledge of linear algebra (matrices, vectors), calculus (derivatives, gradients), and basic probability (Bayes'' rule, distributions).

Note: No prerequisite knowledge of neuroscience is required.','','本课程将计算神经科学重新定位为连接神经科学与下一代人工智能的基础性蓝图。与传统上主要将计算神经科学视为建模生物数据、理解神经机制的工具不同，本课程通过演化的视角来桥接神经科学与人工智能。沿着大脑智能的演化轨迹 --- 从秀丽隐杆线虫（转向控制）到昆虫（模式识别），再到鱼类（强化学习）、小鼠（模拟仿真）、猴类（心智化）以及人类（语言）--- 学生将探索生物系统对计算问题的解决方案如何在历史上启发了现代人工智能，并如何继续推动其未来发展。
本课程围绕当前人工智能系统的关键局限性展开，包括灾难性遗忘、泛化能力差、可解释性缺失以及学习架构僵化等问题。通过从演化和神经科学原理出发分析这些挑战，学生将学会识别诸如可塑性、持续学习机制和可解释表征等设计策略，这些策略有助于构建更鲁棒、更具适应能力的人工智能系统。','This course reframes computational neuroscience as a fundamental blueprint for bridging neuroscience to next-generation artificial intelligence.  Rather than treating it mainly as a tool for modeling biological data and understanding neural mechanisms, this course bridges neuroscience and artificial intelligence through the lens of evolution. Tracing the trajectory of brain intelligence from C. elegans (steering) to insects (pattern recognition), fish (reinforcement learning), mice (simulation), monkeys (mentalizing), and humans (language), students will explore how biological solutions to computational problems have historically inspired - and can continue to advance - modern AI.
The course is organized around key limitations of current AI systems, including catastrophic forgetting, poor generalization, lack of explainability, and rigid learning architectures. By analyzing these challenges through evolutionary and neuroscientific principles, students will learn to identify design strategies - such as plasticity, continual learning mechanisms, and interpretable representations - that can lead to more robust and adaptive artificial systems.','0','0','seed:build_edu_seed','2026-11-15 00:00:00','',NULL,''),
