2026-27 Term 1','GFH1000/GFN1000','GFH1000/GFN1000','本课程包含12周的课程讲授，每周两次课，一次2小时，一次1小时。课程围绕唐宋间中国的吃、穿、住、行、用等生活主题，透过历史载籍、诗文、戏曲、小说、绘画等范围广阔且不失趣味性的多元文献材料，帮助学生对古代的日常生活产生“沉浸式”的体验和理解。此外，课程还会带领学生适度地了解对中国古代日常生活研究的学术论文，理解日常生活孕育出的种种观念在历史中的存在与呈现，以及这些观念是如何影响、指导着具体历史中的人类生活，能进一步思考来自国家与社会的型塑力量与个人欲望之间的微妙互动关系。','This course consists of 12 weeks of lecture, with two sessions per week — one 2-hour session and one 1-hour session. The course revolves around themes of daily life in China during the Tang and Song dynasties, including food, clothing, housing, transportation, and material use. Through a wide-ranging and engaging array of primary sources — including historical texts, poetry and prose, drama, fiction, and painting — the course aims to help students develop an "immersive" experience and understanding of everyday life in ancient China. In addition, the course will guide students to engage with academic scholarship on the study of daily life in ancient China, to understand the various concepts and values that emerged from everyday life and how they manifested across history, and to explore how these concepts shaped and directed human life in specific historical contexts. Students will be further encouraged to reflect on the subtle interplay between the forces of state and society on the one hand, and individual desires on the other.','0','0','seed:build_edu_seed','2026-11-15 00:00:00','',NULL,''),
(101564,'GEC2113','流行病：全球史','','GEC - GE Areas: Area C','2113','G',3.00,2000,'GenEd','已评分','讲演','Main Campus','人文社科学院','人文社科学院','saved_outline','saved_terms','2021-22 Term 2
2022-23 Term 1
2023-24 Term 2
2025-26 Term 1
2021-22 Summer','GFH1000/GFN1000','GFH1000/GFN1000','本课程将从黑死病到2019年新型冠状病毒的爆发来分析流行病对社会和文化的影响。包括的主题有：传染病及其对社会的影响；公共卫生措施的发展；大众歇斯底里和暴力的社会反应；以及新出现和重新出现的疾病问题。我们将讨论流行性疾病暴发与其文化、社会和政治背景之间的复杂历史关系，描述在不同的环境、社会和经济条件下人们和社区与流行病共存的经历，分析在人口、思想和商业流动全球背景下的流行病历史。学生将学习到不同的国家如何以及为什么会以不同的方式应对传染病的爆发, 确定经济、贸易、劳动力流动、性别和阶级等更大的结构性因素与传染病爆发的关系，以及我们从过去的全球传染病中可以吸取什么教训。','This course consists of an analysis of the impact of epidemic diseases on societies and culture from the Black Death to COVID-19 outbreak. Themes include: infectious diseases and their impact on society; the development of public health measures; the social reactions of mass hysteria and violence; and the issue of emerging and re-emerging diseases. We will discuss the complex historical relations between epidemic disease outbreaks and their cultural, social and political context, describe the experiences of populations and communities living with epidemics under diverse environmental, social and economic conditions, analyse the history of epidemics within a global context of movements of people, ideas and commerce. Students will learn how and why different countries have responded to infectious disease outbreaks in different ways, identify how larger structural factors, such as the economy, trade, labor movements, gender and class, are related to an epidemic disease outbreak, and what lessons can be learnt from global pandemics in the past.','0','0','seed:build_edu_seed','2026-11-15 00:00:00','',NULL,''),
(101565,'GEC2114','香港史','','GEC - GE Areas: Area C','2114','G',3.00,2000,'GenEd','已评分','讲演','Main Campus','人文社科学院','人文社科学院','saved_outline','saved_terms','2022-23 Term 2
2024-25 Term 2','GFH1000/GFN1000','GFH1000/GFN1000','在中国历史上，香港是一座独特的城市。鸦片战争之后，香港在一百多年的时间里从一个海边的小渔村发展成为一座国际化都市。这是一座保留着中国传统的现代化城市，也是一座融入世界体系的华人城市。中华传统文化、现代化、殖民主义、后殖民主义都是理解这座城市的关键词。本课程将会介绍香港一百多年在政治、经济、文化等方面的发展历史，讨论香港在城市发展过程中所遭遇的种种挑战与应对机制，进而透过香港理解近代中国与世界的交流与联系。','Hong Kong is a unique city in the history of China. After the Opium War, Hong Kong developed from a small fishing village to a cosmopolitan city in the recent 100 years. It is a modern city that retains its Chinese traditions and a Chinese city that is integrated into the world system. Traditional Chinese culture, modernization, colonialism, and post-colonialism are all key words to understand this city. This course will introduce the history of Hong Kong''s political, economic, and cultural development, discuss the challenges and coping mechanisms that Hong Kong has encountered in the process of urban development, and then understand the connections and communications between modern China and the world through Hong Kong.','0','0','seed:build_edu_seed','2026-11-15 00:00:00','',NULL,''),
(101566,'GEC2115','拉丁美洲历史透视：过去的600年','','GEC - GE Areas: Area C','2115','G',3.00,2000,'GenEd','已评分','讲演','Main Campus','人文社科学院','人文社科学院','saved_outline','saved_terms','2023-24 Term 2
2024-25 Term 2
2025-26 Term 2','GFH1000/GFN1000','GFH1000/GFN1000','该课程旨在向学生介绍拉丁美洲的历史，为他们提供对该地区过去600年的简要而全面的概述。我们的目标是通过讨论这些主题如何密切相关以及我们不能在没有对其根源有清晰理解的情况下理解当代拉丁美洲，从而可能揭开其殖民历史、欠发达和当代争议政治的神秘面纱。

该课程还希望向学生介绍该地区的地理、文化和社会复杂性，重点阅读和讨论一些主要资料，包括日记、文学作品、电影和音乐。

通过这门课程，学生们有望得出结论：拉丁美洲的现实并不像他们可能期望的那样陌生。','The course aims to introduce students to the history of Latin America, providing them with a brief, yet all-encompassing overview of the region’s past 600 years. We aim to potentially demystify ideas on its colonial past, underdevelopment, and contemporary contentious politics by discussing just how these themes are intimately connected and how we cannot understand contemporary Latin America without a clear grasp on its roots.
The course also hopes to introduce students to the region’s geography, culture, and social complexities, focusing readings and discussions on primary sources, from diaries and literary works to movies and music.
Through this course, students will hopefully come to the conclusion that the reality of Latin America is one that is not as foreign to them as they might have expected.','0','0','seed:build_edu_seed','2026-11-15 00:00:00','',NULL,''),
(101567,'GEC2116','近代中国物质文化史','','GEC - GE Areas: Area C','2116','G',3.00,2000,'GenEd','已评分','讲演','Main Campus','人文社科学院','人文社科学院','saved_outline','saved_terms','2024-25 Term 2
2025-26 Term 1
2026-27 Term 1','GFH1000/GFN1000','GFH1000/GFN1000','本课程主要介绍近代中国的物质文化（从晚清时期到二十世纪早期）。课程内容主要被置于历史学的框架内，涉及近代中国社会创造或再创造出的诸多“物件”, 包括鞋子、砚台一类的日常用品，也包括打字机、铁路这类现代设备。本课程的主要目标是让学生初步熟悉物质文化史的主要课题与方法。通过对材料的阅读以及参与课堂活动，学生将探索一系列 “物件”的历史，了解其背后的历史背景及其所留下的文化遗产。','In this course, we will explore the field of material culture in modern China, spanning from the late imperial period to the early twentieth century. Situated within the discipline of history, our exploration encompasses objects shaped or re-invented by the Chinese society, ranging from everyday items like shoes and inkstones to modern innovations such as typewriter and railroads. The primary goal of this course is to familiarize students with key themes, questions, and debates within the intersection of history and material culture. Through readings and class engagements, students will unravel the narratives surrounding selected objects, alongside their historical contexts and cultural heritage.','0','0','seed:build_edu_seed','2026-11-15 00:00:00','',NULL,''),
(101568,'GEC2118','治愈帝国：医学、权力和殖民主义（1600-1914）','','GEC - GE Areas: Area C','2118','G',3.00,2000,'GenEd','已评分','讲演','Main Campus','人文社科学院','人文社科学院','saved_outline','saved_terms','2025-26 Term 1
2026-27 Term 1','GFH1000/GFN1000','GFH1000/GFH1000','医学如何影响帝国的兴衰？帝国如何改变我们理解健康和疾病的方式？本课程探讨殖民扩张时代医学、科学和权利之间错综复杂的关系。从对抗热带疾病到本土治疗传统与西方医学之间的冲突，本课程将探索健康如何成为控制、抵抗和生存的工具。 
通过医生、治疗师、传教士和病人的故事，我们将研究医学的发展如何被用来证明帝国扩张的合理性，被用来强化种族和性别等级制度，并引发反殖民运动。在学习这门课的过程中，我们将把这些历史过程与当代全球医疗卫生所面临的挑战联系起来，揭示殖民医学的遗产仍然如何影响着当今的世界。本课程修读无需具备专业历史或医学知识。','How did medicine shape the rise and fall of empires? And how did empires transform the way we understand health and disease? This course explores the fascinating and complex intersections of medicine, science, and imperial power during the age of colonial expansion. From the fight against tropical diseases to the clash between indigenous healing traditions and Western medicine, we’ll uncover how health became a tool for control, resistance, and survival. 

Through compelling stories of doctors, healers, missionaries, and patients, we will examine how medicine was used to justify empire-building, reinforce racial and gendered hierarchies, and spark anti-colonial movements. Along the way, we’ll connect these historical struggles to modern global health challenges, revealing how the legacy of colonial medicine still shapes the world today. 

This course is designed for students of all backgrounds—no prior knowledge of history or medicine is required.','0','0','seed:build_edu_seed','2026-11-15 00:00:00','',NULL,''),
(101569,'GEC2119','中华文明的形成','','GEC - GE Areas: Area C','2119','G',3.00,2000,'GenEd','已评分','讲演','Main Campus','人文社科学院','人文社科学院','saved_outline','saved_terms','2026-27 Term 1','GFH1000/GFN1000','GFH1000/GFN1000','本课程从史料运用与制度变迁的角度考察早期至中古中国，以考古发现、出土文献和传世文本为主要线索，说明早期中国如何在不同材料中被重建与理解。课程继而讨论周代政治秩序如何进入秦汉帝国制度的框架，并进一步考察中古时期贵族秩序、国家权力与社会经济结构的重组，最后引出唐宋变革与近世中国的形成。本课程不要求学生具备中国史基础。','This course examines early to medieval China through historical evidence and institutional change. It introduces archaeology, excavated manuscripts, and transmitted texts as key sources for reconstructing early history. It then follows the shift from Zhou political order to Qin-Han empire, and the later transformation of aristocratic order, state power, and social-economic structure. The course ends with the Tang-Song transition and the emergence of early modern China. No prior background in Chinese history is required.','0','0','seed:build_edu_seed','2026-11-15 00:00:00','',NULL,''),
(101570,'GEC2120','反乌托邦','','GEC - GE Areas: Area C','2120','G',3.00,2000,'GenEd','已评分','讲演','Main Campus','人文社科学院','人文社科学院','saved_outline','saved_terms','2026-27 Term 1','GFH1000/GFN1000','GFH1000/GFN1000','本课程以反乌托邦题材为重点，考察世界各地的文学和电影作品，以研究和理解引发人们对未来的焦虑的历史、社会、政治和文化背景。','This course focuses on the genre of dystopia, surveying examples in literature and film from around the world in order to study and understand the historical, social, political, and cultural contexts that give rise to these anxieties about the future.','0','0','seed:build_edu_seed','2026-11-15 00:00:00','',NULL,''),
