2025-26 Term 2
2023-24 Summer','GFH1000/GFN1000','GFH1000/GFN1000','本课程将介绍国际关系和比较政治学中的主要参与者、议题、进程以及试图解释这些参与者、议题和进程的理论。我们将运用理论分析世界政治中的案例与现象，并且探索不同理论在解释这些案例和现象时的有效性问题。课程探讨的一些重要话题有：权力与世界秩序、国家及其在世界政治中的角色、战争与和平、核武器扩散与美国外交政策、全球时代中的经济、西方面临的挑战、中东与西方、全球的未来。','This course is an introduction to the major actors, issues and processes of international relations and comparative politics and the theories that attempt to explain them. Students will be encouraged to use theory as a methodological tool for analysing cases and phenomena in world politics and explore the efficacy of different theoretical approaches in explaining these phenomena. Important topics that the course deals with are: power and world order, the state in world politics, war and peace, nuclear proliferation and American foreign policy, the economy in a global age, challenges to the West, the Middle East and the West, and global futures.','0','0','seed:build_edu_seed','2026-11-15 00:00:00','',NULL,''),
(101577,'GEC2208','理想主义抑或实用主义：比较视野中的政府','','GEC - GE Areas: Area C','2208','G',3.00,2000,'GenEd','已评分','讲演','Main Campus','人文社科学院','人文社科学院','saved_outline','saved_terms','2026-27 Term 1','GFH1000/GFN1000','GFH1000/GFN1000','为什么即使同为民主国家，不同国家的政治制度也会产生不同的政治结果？与美国相比，英国和日本更频繁地更换政府首脑。美国总统，如唐纳德·特朗普，可能会遭到弹劾，但很难被免职；相比之下，多位韩国总统在卸任后曾面临刑事起诉。在新西兰，较小政党可以在组建政府过程中发挥关键作用，有时甚至能够使一个主要政党执政，而另一个主要政党成为反对党。这些差异的原因是什么？
本课程将通过比较不同国家的政府形式、民主制度、选举制度和政党制度来探讨这些问题。课程将考察宪法、联邦制与单一制、总统制、议会制、半总统制、选举规则以及政党竞争如何塑造政治结果。
本课程的案例研究将包括英国、美国、德国、新西兰、加拿大、法国、韩国、日本、印度和以色列。通过这些比较，学生将学习制度设计如何影响政治结果。课程结束时，学生将理解比较政府研究中的关键争论，批判性地评估不同政治制度的优势与弱点，并形成更广阔的跨国政治理解。','Why do political institutions produce different outcomes across countries, even among democracies? Compared with America, the UK and Japan change their heads of government frequently. In the United States, presidents such as Donald Trump may be impeached but are difficult to remove from office, whereas several South Korean presidents have faced criminal prosecution after leaving office. In New Zealand, smaller parties can play a decisive role in forming governments, sometimes enabling one major party to govern while another remains in opposition. What are the causes of these differences?
This course examines these questions by comparing forms of government, democratic institutions, electoral systems, and party systems across a range of countries. We will explore how constitutions, federal and unitary systems, presidentialism, parliamentarism, semi-presidentialism, electoral rules, and party competition shape political outcomes.
Case studies will include the United Kingdom, the United States, Germany, New Zealand, Canada, France, South Korea, Japan, India, and Israel. Through these comparisons, students will learn how institutional design affects political outcomes. By the end of the course, students will understand key debates in comparative government, critically evaluate the strengths and weaknesses of different political systems, and develop a broader cross-national understanding of politics.','0','0','seed:build_edu_seed','2026-11-15 00:00:00','',NULL,''),
