2026-27 Term 1','GFH1000/GFN1000','GFH1000/GFN1000','本课程概述西方哲学史上各主要时期对西方文明产生过根本影响的人物和运动。内容涵盖古希腊、中世纪以及近现代哲学。','This course provides an overview of the figures and movements of the canonical periods in the history of Western Philosophy that have had a fundamental influence on Western Civilization. Periods to be covered include Ancient, Hellenistic and Medieval, and Modern philosophy.','0','0','seed:build_edu_seed','2026-11-15 00:00:00','',NULL,''),
(101637,'GED2123','中国哲学史','','GED - GE Areas: Area D','2123','G',3.00,2000,'GenEd','已评分','讲演','Main Campus','人文社科学院','人文社科学院','saved_outline','saved_terms','2023-24 Term 1
2024-25 Term 1
2024-25 Term 2
2025-26 Term 1
2025-26 Term 2
2026-27 Term 1','GFH1000/GFN1000','GFH1000/GFN1000','本课程涵盖中国自先秦洎明代之主要哲学思想。课程前半
段，首论先秦之孔子、孟子、荀子、老子、庄子、墨子与韩非子
诸家，续探汉儒董仲舒与魏晋玄学。课程后半段，介绍大乘佛教
思想（中观之学、唯识之论）、中国佛教三宗（天台宗、法华宗、禅宗）与玄奘法相宗，再厘探宋明儒学发展，聚焦周敦颐、
张载、邵雍、程颢、程颐、朱熹、陆九渊与王阳明。
本课程着重哲学问题之探讨与分析，亦结合文本精读与脉络
研究，由下列学术视角研析中国哲学主要思想与著作，期许学生
(1)掌握传统注疏与经学史, (2) 理解哲学史与思想史脉络, (3) 探索
哲学思想中之人生价值、社会理念与政治蓝图，尤为重要的是 (4)
培养对哲学文本中核心概念、哲学体系、思维模式与论证逻辑之
解析能力。
课程讨论议题包括人生意义、社会价值、道德哲学、心灵哲
学、伦理学、政治哲学、宇宙论、形上学、知识论、宗教、思想
史、政治文化史。课程阅读材料包含中国哲学文献原典与重要学
术研究，勖勉诸生从不同学术取径与视角研读国故。','This course provides a survey of Chinese philosophy from the Pre-Qin period (pre-221 BC) to the Ming period (1368-1644). The first part of this course examines Pre-Qin philosophers like Confucius, Mencius, Xun Zi, Lao Zi, Zhuang Zi, Hanfei Zi and Mo Zi, and deals with Han (206 BC–220 AD) Confucian Dong Zhongsu and Wei-Jin Metaphysics (220-420). After the mid-term, this course first examines Buddhism with a focus on the Mahāyāna School (Madhyamaka and Yogācāra), three Chinese Buddhist Schools (Tiantai, Huayan and Chan), Xuan Zang’s Consciousness-Only School as well as seminal Buddhist Sutras in China. Next, we investigate Song-Ming Confucianism (960-1276; 1368-1644) with emphasis on Zhou Dunyi, Zhang Zai, Shao Yong, Cheng Hao, Cheng Yi, Zhu Xi, Lu Jiuyuan and Wang Yangming. As to academic approaches, this course concentrates on philosophical analysis as well as combines contextual and textual studies. We study the major works of Chinese philosophers through the following approaches: (1) a solid understanding of the traditional exegesis and the history of classics, hermeneutics and canonization, (2) a study of the contexts of intellectual history and the history of philosophy, (3) an investigation of the values concerning life, society and politics in philosophical texts, and (4), most importantly, an analysis of central concepts, the system of philosophy, the mode of thought and the logic of argumentation. The topics we discuss include the meaning of life, social values, moral philosophy, philosophy of mind, ethics, political philosophy, cosmology, metaphysics, epistemology, religion, intellectual history, history of political culture, etc. The readings of this course are mainly the primary and secondary sources of Chinese philosophy in Chinese.','0','0','seed:build_edu_seed','2026-11-15 00:00:00','',NULL,''),
(101638,'GED2124','西方世界中的种族问题：议题、观念和框架','','GED - GE Areas: Area D','2124','G',3.00,2000,'GenEd','已评分','讲演','Main Campus','人文社科学院','人文社科学院','saved_outline','saved_terms','2023-24 Term 2
2024-25 Term 1
2024-25 Term 2
2025-26 Term 2
2026-27 Term 1','GFH1000/GFN1000','GFH1000/GFN1000','该课程探讨了西方世界中种族的观念和现实，为学生介绍了这些多方面、复杂的辩论。课程旨在利用理论工作和以身体经验为基础的文学作品，引发学生对种族类别、种族主义和自我认知意义的思考。基于种族是社会建构而非固有事实的观点，课程将不断引导学生质疑在由我们并非完全掌控的概念框架下，我们是谁以及这意味着什么。为了帮助学生理解可能有些陌生的观念，课程将以整体的主题方法来探讨该课题，重点关注国家、科学和流行文化等几个主要议题，并提供以特定国家和事件为焦点的案例研究，以便在需要时进行深入探讨。例如，我们将讨论种族观念在后殖民主义努力中对国家叙事的作用，科学种族主义以及人类头骨大小与智力相关的神话的起源，第二次世界大战的叙事，以及为何在过去十年里卷发成为美洲地区美容业的宠儿等话题。','The course explores both the ideas and realities of race in the western world, serving as an introduction for students to these multifaceted and complex debates. Making use of both theoretical work and literature that builds on embodied experiences, the course aims to engage students’ own perspectives on the meanings of racial categories, racism, and self-perception. Building on the notion that race is a social construct and not a given, the course will constantly make students question what it means to be who we are when the world that we live in is framed by concepts we don’t necessarily have control over. To hone students’ ability to grasp ideas that are perhaps a bit too foreign, the course will take a grand thematical approach to the subject, focusing on a few main topics such as the state, science, and popular culture; and providing case studies that will focus on particular nations and episodes, allowing us an in-depth approach when needed. We will, for instance, discuss the role race – as an idea - played in postcolonial efforts to produce national narratives, how scientific racism and the myth that humans’ skull size could indicate intelligence was born, the narratives behind World War II, and why in the past decade curly hair became a beauty industry darling in the Americas.','0','0','seed:build_edu_seed','2026-11-15 00:00:00','',NULL,''),
(101639,'GED2125','人工智能伦理学','','GED - GE Areas: Area D','2125','G',3.00,2000,'GenEd','已评分','讲演','Main Campus','人文社科学院','人文社科学院','saved_outline','saved_terms','2025-26 Term 2','GFH1000/GFN1000','GFH1000/GFN1000','从疾病检测（包括癌症研究）领域的应用前景，到人类劳动的本质及对人类劳动的需求的根本性变革；从对真实与虚假界限模糊的担忧，到关乎"何以为人"的本质性追问——人工智能技术正迫使人类不得不快速思考诸多紧迫的伦理议题，无论我们是否已做好准备。本课程旨在通过锤炼学生的批判性思维能力，使其能够理解、分析并应对人工智能多样化应用带来的紧迫问题。课程将探讨AI开发与使用过程中的各类伦理挑战，并引入道德哲学理论工具来解析这些问题。具体议题包括：数据隐私保护、劳动力替代危机、各种算法偏见、AI生成内容的真实可靠性、大语言模型在医疗/教育领域的应用、AI运行的环境可持续性与能源消耗，以及新兴AI形态可能引发的存在主义威胁等。','This course is designed to provide students with an opportunity to hone their critical thinking skills in order to be able to understand, analyze, and address pressing issues relating to various forms of implementation of AI. Students will learn about various ethical challenges related to AI development and use and be introduced to theories in moral philosophy that will be helpful in understanding and addressing those problems. Examples of issues to be explored in the course include data privacy, human worker displacement, various forms of algorithmic bias, truth and reliability in connection with AI, the use of LLMs in medical or therapeutic settings, the use of LLMs in education, environmental sustainability and powering AI, and existential threats potentially brought on by emerging and anticipated new forms of AI.','0','0','seed:build_edu_seed','2026-11-15 00:00:00','',NULL,''),
(101640,'GED2126','哲学探讨: 爱的概念','','GED - GE Areas: Area D','2126','G',3.00,2000,'GenEd','已评分','讲演','Main Campus','人文社科学院','人文社科学院','saved_outline','saved_terms','2025-26 Term 2
2026-27 Term 1','GFH1000/GFN1000','GFH1000/GFN1000','这是一门围绕爱展开哲学探讨的课程。通过研读历史文献与当代著述，我们将探讨伦理学、政治哲学、形而上学、语言哲学、文化研究与认识论等哲学领域的重要议题。例如，我们将剖析爱作为心理与社会双重现象的本质，解读其错综复杂的特性。此课程旨在培养哲学核心素养：理解与重构论证、评估与批判论点、提升个人论证能力。同时获得的思维工具，或许能助学生审视自身情感生活与周遭人际关系的脉络。

本课程着重培养人文学科核心能力，包括：文本细读与批判能力、清晰写作与严密论证能力、高效参与小组讨论能力。课程结束时，您将系统掌握以下通用技能：（一）如何研读深奥哲学文献；（二）如何识别并重构论证框架；（三）如何构建并清晰表述个人论点，涵盖书面表达与课堂口头陈述','This is a course on philosophical topics surrounding love. We will touch on issues in all areas of philosophy including ethics, political philosophy, metaphysics, philosophy of language, cultural studies and epistemology by examining both historical and contemporary texts. We will, for example, address the nature of love both as a mental and social phenomenon and understand the complexities of love. You will develop the sorts of skills fundamental to philosophy: understanding and reconstructing arguments, evaluating and criticizing arguments, and developing your own argumentative abilities. You will also acquire theoretical tools that might be useful for thinking about your own love lives, and the lives of those around you.
This class focuses on developing skills that are central to humanities classes. These include: skills in reading a text closely and critically, skills in writing clearly and in constructing a well-defended argument, and skills in productive group discussion. By the end of this course, you will have developed and refined the following general skills: (i) how to read difficult philosophical material; (ii) how to identify and reconstruct arguments; and (iii) how to produce and clearly articulate your own arguments, both in writing and orally in class.','0','0','seed:build_edu_seed','2026-11-15 00:00:00','',NULL,''),
