2025-26 Term 2','GFH1000/GFN1000','GFH1000/GFN1000','本课程以全球视角和比较方法介绍宗教哲学，探讨佛教、中国宗教（儒教、道教）、基督教、印度教、伊斯兰教和犹太教等各种宗教传统的原因和合理性。课程主要议题分为三个方面：人类处境、终极实在和宗教真理。','This course is double coded with PHI2202. This course introduces philosophy of religion with a global perspective and a comparative approach. It will explore the reasons and rationalities embedded in a variety of religious traditions including Buddhism, Chinese religions (Confucianism and Daoism), Christianity, Hinduism, Islam and Judaism. The main topics will fall into three areas: the human condition, ultimate realities, and religious truth.','0','0','seed:build_edu_seed','2026-11-15 00:00:00','',NULL,''),
(101643,'GED2301','文学与人性','','GED - GE Areas: Area D','2301','G',3.00,2000,'GenEd','已评分','讲演','Main Campus','人文社科学院','人文社科学院','saved_outline','saved_terms','2019-20 Summer
2020-21 Summer
2022-23 Summer','GFH1000/GFN1000','GFH1000/GFN1000','本课程将向学生展示英语文学的世界，内容涵盖小说、诗歌和戏剧作品。我们将分析每部文学作品在当时和在我们时代的意义。通过课程的学习，学生将在文学欣赏的过程中习得相关技巧和语言技能。我们鼓励学生阅读不同时期和不同文化背景的文学作品，以开阔和加深对人类经验的理解。本课程无需相关课程基础。','Double coded with HSS2010.
This course introduces you to the world of literature in English, covering works of fiction, poetry and drama. We will analyze the significance of each literary work for its time and for our time. In the process, you will acquire techniques and language skills for literary appreciation. You are encouraged to read literary works from different time periods and different cultural backgrounds to broaden and deepen your understanding of the human experience. No prerequisite course is required.','0','0','seed:build_edu_seed','2026-11-15 00:00:00','',NULL,''),
(101644,'GED2302','《哈姆雷特》：问题的诠释','','GED - GE Areas: Area D','2302','G',3.00,2000,'GenEd','已评分','讲演','Main Campus','人文社科学院','人文社科学院','saved_outline','saved_terms','2021-22 Term 1
2021-22 Term 2
2022-23 Term 1
2023-24 Term 1
2024-25 Term 1
2025-26 Term 1
2026-27 Term 1','GFH1000/GFN1000','GFH1000/GFN1000','本课程探讨了与威廉·莎士比亚的悲剧《哈姆雷特》的相关学术问题。课程涉及范围广泛，因为《哈姆雷特》还将作为扩展案例研究，让学生在该剧基础上探索不同的哲学观点。《哈姆雷特》被认为是仅次于《圣经》的第二大研究最多的英语文本。该剧自 1601 年以来就被广为表演沿用。

本课程中，学生将探索这部戏剧的内容、情节、人物塑造和比喻语言，以及一系列由黑格尔到汉斯·伽达默尔、阿多诺和卡尔施密特等著名理论家和当代女权主义读物提出的关于其含义的理论观点。课程只选择相对直接且易于理解的摘录。这些简短的摘录也将在讲演（Lecture）中进行总结，帮助学生理解。学习这些摘录不需要完全理解理论家的整体立场，学生将在讲演中习得基本的总结和释义，以帮助理解。课堂上，学生将围绕这部戏剧进行阅读、讨论和撰写，并使用广泛的哲学和批判理论文本评估各种评论家的观点。其中，学生将探索对整部戏剧意义的竞争性解释进行批判分析的要求，以及浪漫主义对存在主义问题发展的特征和中心地位。学生还将探索由卡尔施密特和其他人的现实主义方法提出的宪法问题，将这些问题与拉康、琼斯等人提出的关于哈姆雷特的心理/精神分析问题进行对比。在这方面，哈姆雷特作为一个扩展案例研究来说明一系列解释性立场和理论立场的特征和可能的局限性。学生还将探讨该剧的一些文学和语言层面，包括其比喻语言的作用和挑战。

阅读材料将选自有影响力的评论家，包括对《哈姆雷特》彼此的解释提出异议的评论家们。重点将放在学生如何在写作和课堂展示中，表现出对一手和二手文本进行直观、分析和批判地反应的能力。','This course addresses various academic and geopolitical issues arising from a reflective encounter with the tragedy Hamlet by William Shakespeare. Hamlet is claimed to be the second most studied English language text second only to the Bible. The play itself is reported to have been in performance somewhere in the world continuously since 1601. This course is wide-ranging in scope because Hamlet also serves as an extended case study allowing students to encounter and explore different philosophical and geopolitical standpoints on specific aspects and challenging themes raised by this play.
This course invites students to explore the contents of this play, its plotline, action, scheming, characterisation and figurative language. After watching the entire film version of the play, the final part of the course also encourages students to analyse different theoretical perspectives upon its implications as developed by a selection of eminent theorists ranging from Hegel through to Hans Gadamer, Adorno and Carl Schmitt and contemporary feminist readings. Only extracts of these theorists that are relatively straightforward and open to a clear paraphrase will be selected for study. These short extracts will also be summarised in lectures to aid with their comprehension at undergraduate class level. These extracts do not require a full understanding of the theorist’s overall positions and students will be provided with a basic summary and paraphrase in the lectures and PPTs to aid their comprehension.
Students will be required to read, discuss and write about the full text of this play using the approach of “close reading,” while also later giving their assessment of the perspectives taken on Hamlet by a variety of commentators and critics using a wide range of texts in philosophy and critical theory. In particular, students will explore the requirements of a critical analysis of competing interpretations of the significance of the entire play. Students will also explore constitutional and geopolitical issues developed by realist approaches of Carl Schmitt, Hegel and others, contrasting these with the psychological/psychoanalytical questions about Hamlet as developed by Lacan and Jones. In this respect, Hamlet serves as an extended running case study to illustrate both the character and possible limits of a range of interpretive standpoints and theoretical positions. Students will also address some literary and linguistic dimensions of this play including the role and challenges of its figurative language operating as literary devices vital to the expression of a range of sometimes enigmatic meanings.
Emphasis will be placed on students’ capacity to respond intuitively, analytically, as well as critically, to the primary and secondary texts in their written work and presentations.','0','0','seed:build_edu_seed','2026-11-15 00:00:00','',NULL,''),
(101645,'GED2303','重访唐诗：温故知新之旅','','GED - GE Areas: Area D','2303','G',3.00,2000,'GenEd','已评分','讲演','Main Campus','人文社科学院','人文社科学院','saved_outline','saved_terms','2025-26 Term 1
2026-27 Term 1','GFH1000/GFN1000','GFH1000/GFN1000','唐代是中国诗歌史的巅峰时代，名篇和名家辈出；与此同时，唐代也是中国古典小说发展的重要阶段，文人创作的传奇小说与唐诗相互辉映，共同构成了这一时代璀璨的文学图景。每一个中国人在不同的人生阶段都曾以教育、阅读、娱乐等不同方式接触和学习过唐诗。本课分为12周左右，每周包括2小时的唐诗讲论和1小时的唐传奇导读。2小时的讲论部分将通过都城、事业、边塞、离合、游览、节令、爱情、无题等主题，带领同学重新阅读诗歌史上最经典的篇章，了解唐诗的发展脉络、主要流派及代表诗人，掌握唐诗的艺术特色。1小时的唐传奇阅读，将带领同学了解《霍小玉传》《莺莺传》《李娃传》《柳氏传》《任氏传》《柳毅传》《枕中记》《杜子春》等名篇，最终理解唐诗与唐代社会、政治、文化的互动关系，通过叙事了解抒情，通过抒情深化叙事，以及最终通过文学来理解自己。','The Tang dynasty represents the pinnacle of Chinese poetry, producing an abundance of masterworks and celebrated poets. At the same time, it was also a formative period in the development of Chinese classical fiction, in which the literary tales known as chuanqi (传奇) flourished alongside Tang poetry, together forming the brilliant literary landscape of the age. Every Chinese person, at different stages of life, has encountered and studied Tang poetry through education, reading, or popular culture. This course spans approximately 12 weeks, with each week comprising a 2-hour lecture on Tang poetry and a 1-hour guided reading of Tang chuanqi tales. The 2-hour lecture sessions will guide students to re-read the most celebrated poems in the history of Chinese literature through thematic lenses — including the capital city, career and ambition, the frontier, separation and reunion, travel, seasonal festivals, love, and feelings beyond words — while tracing the historical development of Tang poetry, its major schools and representative poets, and its distinctive artistic characteristics. The 1-hour chuanqi reading sessions will introduce students to celebrated tales including The Story of Huo Xiaoyu, The Story of Yingying, The Story of Li Wa, The Story of Lady Liu, The Story of Ren, The Story of Liu Yi, The World Inside a Pillow, and Du Zichun. Together, the two components aim to illuminate the interaction between Tang poetry and Tang society, politics, and culture — understanding lyric through narrative, and deepening narrative through lyric — and ultimately, to understand oneself through literature.','0','0','seed:build_edu_seed','2026-11-15 00:00:00','',NULL,''),
(101646,'GED2401','电影艺术百年史','','GED - GE Areas: Area D','2401','G',3.00,2000,'GenEd','已评分','讲演','Main Campus','人文社科学院','人文社科学院','ghost_outline_unavailable','',NULL,'','',NULL,NULL,'0','0','seed:build_edu_seed','2026-11-15 00:00:00','',NULL,''),
(101647,'GED2402','电影类型与情感','','GED - GE Areas: Area D','2402','G',3.00,2000,'GenEd','已评分','讲演','Main Campus','人文社科学院','人文社科学院','saved_outline','saved_terms','2021-22 Term 1
2021-22 Term 2
2022-23 Term 1
2022-23 Term 2
2023-24 Term 1
2023-24 Term 2','GFH1000/GFN1000','GFH1000/GFN1000','电影的类型对电影文化至关重要。它们为编剧和导演提供拍摄新电影的既定思路。它们使工作室制作人和制片人做计划有迹可循，并相信这套模式会吸引观众。但也许最有趣的是，类型是与潜在观众的一种契约。电影海报和预告片中熟悉的图像标志着它会在观众身上创造出一种情感体验，以换取他们的金额回报。也许比任何媒体都更重要的是，电影与刺激观众愉悦的强烈感觉有关。
本课程探讨某些类型的电影，如何以及为什么尽管它们的内容通常是可预测的，但始终能激起观众的情绪。 关于“如何”的部分，我们会探讨常规主题、故事和风格技巧。电影制作人如何打动观众，他们的策略是如何随着时间和不同文化背景而改变的？ 关于“为什么”的部分，课程会涉及美学与人类心理之间更深层次的关系。什么是情绪，它们与我们的思想和身体有什么关系？虚构的故事和艺术作品如何激发情感？它们与我们在日常生活中感受到的相同还是不同？为什么人们对他们知道不是真实的角色和情况有如此强烈的感觉？为什么有人会自愿选择感到恐惧、悲伤或厌恶？——虽然他们通常不惜一切代价避免这些情绪。
本课程用三种电影类型来回答这些问题，每种类型都直接专注于激发特定的情绪反应。 “耸人听闻的情节剧”——我们今天最熟悉的动作片——在现代作为一种戏剧类型出现，通过制造恐惧和悬念来刺激观众。在一个曲折的历史发展过程中，“情节剧”这个词后来用来指代一组非常不同的电影——故事与家庭和家庭密切相关，并且通常在制作时考虑到女性的喜好。 “哭泣”情节剧或“催泪者”激发了悲情，让观众有机会“好好哭一场”——尽管许多观众发现自己在哭泣时羞于承认。最后，好莱坞借鉴了西方浪漫主义的传统，开创了其最经久不衰的类型之一：恐怖片。一个多世纪以来，观众蜂拥而至这些电影，不仅要体验恐惧，还要体验让他们在身体和道德上都反感的厌恶。
当我们讨论每个流派时，我们会考虑准确定义流派的困难性，每个流派可能存在的多样性，以及它们如何随着时间的推移而转变，并始终借鉴其他文化和艺术传统。每周放映会播放样片电影，而导修课程则侧重于探索艺术和情感的理论文本。该课程文献将涉及心理学、人类学、哲学和认知科学。它还将探讨情感与更广泛的美学和道德价值问题之间的关系。情感上强大的艺术作品是否对社会有益，是无害的，还是完全危险的？什么样的道德原则支配着那些试图吸引观众情绪，从而可能影响他们的行为和信仰的艺术家？我们希望不仅通过电影放映来阐述概念，而且希望提供一个环境，让学生在阅读和电影之间建立新的联系。','Genres are essential to movie culture. They provide writers and directors with readymade formulae for new films. They help studio executives and producers plan releases on a schedule with the confidence that they will attract an audience. But perhaps most interestingly, genres serve as a kind of contract with potential viewers. The familiar imagery in a film’s posters and trailers signals the kind of emotional experience it will create in a viewer in exchange for their cash. Perhaps more so than any medium, movies are associated with stimulating powerful feelings for viewers’ pleasure.

This course examines how and why certain genres of film, despite their content often being predictable, consistently elicit viewers’ emotions. The how refers their regular themes, stories, and stylistic techniques. How do filmmakers move viewers, and how have their strategies changed over time and in different cultural contexts? The why refers to the deeper relationship between aesthetics and human psychology. What are emotions, and how do they relate to our minds and bodies? How do fictional stories and artworks provoke emotions? Are they the same or different from the ones we feel in everyday life? Why do people feel so strongly for characters and situations that they know are not real? And why would anyone voluntarily choose to be feel fear, sadness, or disgust—emotions they normally avoid at all costs?

This course uses three genres to take up these questions, each of them directly focused on stimulating a particular emotional response. The “sensational melodrama”—most familiar to us today in the action film—emerged in the modern era as a theatrical genre that thrilled audiences by creating fear and suspense. In a twisting historical development, the word “melodrama” later came to refer to a very different group of films—ones with stories close to the home and family and often crafted with women’s tastes in mind. The “weepie” melodrama or “tearjerker” stimulates pathos and gave the viewer an opportunity to “have a good cry”—even as many viewers find themselves ashamed to admit when they weep. Finally, Hollywood drew on traditions of Western Romanticism as it pioneered one of its most enduring genres: the horror film. Audiences have flocked to these films for over a century to experience not only fear, but also disgust that nauseates them physically and morally.

As we discuss each genre, we consider the difficulties of defining a genre precisely, the diversity possible within each, and how they evolve and transform over time, always drawing on other cultural and artistic traditions. Weekly screenings include exemplary films, while tutorial sessions focus on theoretical texts that explore art and emotion. The course will balance literature from the disciplines of psychology, anthropology, philosophy, and cognitive science. It will also explore the relationship between emotion and broader questions of aesthetic and moral value. Are emotionally powerful artworks socially beneficial, simply harmless, or outright dangerous? And what ethical principles govern artists who seek to engage viewers’ emotions, and therefore possibly their behavior and beliefs? We hope not merely to illustrate concepts through the screenings, but rather to provide an environment in which students draw new connections among the readings and films.','0','0','seed:build_edu_seed','2026-11-15 00:00:00','',NULL,''),
(101648,'GED2403','現當代古典音樂：聆聽、理解與批判','','GED - GE Areas: Area D','2403','G',3.00,2000,'GenEd','已评分','讲演','Main Campus','人文社科学院','人文社科学院','saved_outline','saved_terms','2025-26 Term 1','GFH1000/GFN1000','GFH1000/GFN1000','古典音樂巨匠，如貝多芬、莫札特和蕭邦的名字廣為人知，他們的作品被視為「古典音樂」。但他們都生活在幾個世紀前。如今，現當代作曲家繼承了他們的遺產，創作出我們這個時代的「古典音樂」。本課程旨在引導學生批判性地聆聽現當代古典音樂，探索這些作品在聽感上與傳統古典音樂的不同，並理解這些變化背後的文化和歷史原因。它培養學生對音樂表達之文化、價值觀和信仰的多樣性的欣賞，同時檢視音樂如何反映和塑造社會和文化。
        課程從19世紀末到20世紀初的晚期浪漫主義音樂開始，逐步推進到二戰前、二戰後到千禧年前，以及21世紀。在地理上，課程聚焦於德奧學派和法國學派，同時也涉及其他歐洲國家、美洲和亞洲的音樂。學生將運用批判性方法和理論概念來分析音樂中的文化和歷史問題。課程概述了主要的美學運動，介紹關鍵作曲家及其作品，使學生能夠欣賞現當代古典音樂的豐富多樣性，並培養分析其與社會和文化互動的技能。','The names of classical music giants such as Beethoven, Mozart, and Chopin are widely recognised, and their compositions are considered "classical music," even though they lived centuries ago. Nowadays, modern and contemporary composers carry on their legacy, creating the "classical music" of our time. This course instructs students to listen critically to modern and contemporary classical music, exploring how these works differ from traditional classical music regarding the listening experience and understanding the cultural and historical reasons behind these changes. It nurtures an appreciation for the diversity of cultures, values, and beliefs expressed through music while examining how music reflects and shapes society and culture.
Beginning with late Romantic music from the late 19th to early 20th century, the course advances through pre-World War II, post-World War II to the pre-millennium era, and the 21st century. Geographically, it focuses on the German-Austrian and French schools while also addressing music from other European countries, the Americas, and Asia. Students will apply critical methodologies and theoretical concepts to analyse cultural and historical issues in music. The course offers an overview of major aesthetic movements, introducing key composers and their works, enabling students to appreciate the rich diversity of modern and contemporary classical music and develop the skills to analyse its interaction with society and culture.','0','0','seed:build_edu_seed','2026-11-15 00:00:00','',NULL,''),
(101649,'GED2404','梦想的世纪：1925-2025年舞台与银幕上的音乐剧','','GED - GE Areas: Area D','2404','G',3.00,2000,'GenEd','已评分','讲演','Main Campus','人文社科学院','人文社科学院','saved_outline','saved_terms','2025-26 Term 2
2026-27 Term 1','GFH1000/GFN1000','GFH1000/GFN1000','本课程通过文化史与创作实践的双重视角，对西方音乐剧进行总体介绍。课程将比较和对比现场表演与电影版本的音乐剧，采用多学科方法呈现和分析过去百年间的代表性作品。学生将培养广泛的技能，以理解文字、音乐、舞蹈、设计、戏剧和电影导演在创造音乐剧多维效果中的作用，从而加深对这些独特多样且复杂的流行文化产物的欣赏。','This course serves as a general introduction to musical theater in the West through the dual lenses of cultural history and creative practice. Comparing and contrasting musicals in live performance and film versions, the course takes a multidisciplinary approach to presenting and analyzing representative examples from the past hundred years. Students will develop a broad skillset for understanding the role of words, music, dance, design, theater and film direction in creating the multidimensional effect of musical theater, thereby deepening their appreciation of these uniquely diverse and sophisticated products of popular culture.','0','0','seed:build_edu_seed','2026-11-15 00:00:00','',NULL,''),
(101650,'GED2501','应用认知心理学','','GED - GE Areas: Area D','2501','G',3.00,2000,'GenEd','已评分','讲演','Main Campus','人文社科学院','人文社科学院','saved_outline','saved_terms','2020-21 Summer
2022-23 Summer
2023-24 Summer
2024-25 Summer','GFH1000/GFN1000','GFH1000/GFN1000','认知心理学是研究大脑如何处理信息的。在日常生活中，它是关于获取和利用从我们的感官获得的知识和经验的心理过程。认知的主要过程是知觉、学习、记忆储存、检索和思考。所有这些认知的各个方面都在实验室中得到了广泛的研究，但近年来，人们对认知心理学在现实生活中的应用越来越感兴趣。这种方法被称为应用认知心理学，它关注于研究认知过程如何影响我们在现实生活中的行为和表现。

应用认知心理学家的目标是实现两件事(1)获得可以应用于现实世界的心理学知识和见解，从而产生解决现实问题的方法;(2)从日常生活中了解我们的认知功能，以进一步完善认知的理论模型。

在本课程中，我们将探讨我们的认知能力在现实生活中的作用。我们将讨论日常记忆，比如我们的自传式记忆以及我们的记忆在目击者证词和警察采访中有多可靠;我们将探讨提高记忆性能的各种记忆策略(如间隔记忆、助记法、检索促进和抑制);我们将涵盖随着年龄增长我们的信息处理能力(工作记忆)以及我们如何在一天中做出决定(决策);最后，我们将探讨我们的认知能力是如何受到生物过程(如昼夜节律、月经周期)和各种药物(合法和非法)的影响。','Cognitive psychology is the study of how the brain processes information. In more everyday terms, it is about the mental processes involved in acquiring and making use of knowledge and experience gained from our senses. The main processes involved in cognition are perception, learning, memory storage, retrieval and thinking. All of these various aspects of cognition have been extensively studied in the laboratory, but in recent years there has been a growing interest in the application of cognitive psychology to situations in real life. This approach is known as applied cognitive psychology, and it is concerned with the investigation of how cognitive processes affect our behaviour and performance in real-life settings.Applied cognitive psychologists aim to achieve two things (1) to acquire psychological knowledge and insights that can be used in the real world and thus produce solutions to real problems; and (2) to providing everyday life insights on our cognitive functioning to further improve theoretical models of cognition.   In this course we will look at how our cognitive abilities function in real-life situations. We will discuss everyday memory such as our autobiographical memory and how reliable our memory is in regard to eyewitness testimonies and police interviews; We will look at various memory strategies to improve memory performance (e.g., spacing, mnemonics, retrieval facilitation and inhibition); We will cover our information-processing abilities (working memory) as we age and how we make decisions throughout the day (decision making); Finally we will look at how our cognitive abilities are affected by biological processes (e.g. circadian rhythms, menstrual cycle) and various types of drugs (legal and illegal).','0','0','seed:build_edu_seed','2026-11-15 00:00:00','',NULL,''),
(101651,'GED2502','大学生成长与心理健康：新兴成年期的发展视角','','GED - GE Areas: Area D','2502','G',3.00,2000,'GenEd','已评分','讲演','Main Campus','人文社科学院','人文社科学院','saved_outline','saved_terms','2026-27 Term 1','GFH1000/GFN1000','GFH1000/GFN1000','大学生成长与心理健康：新兴成年期的发展视角','Emerging adulthood, spanning the late teens through the twenties, is a unique developmental period marked by significant opportunities, transitions, and personal growth. This course explores how developmental experiences, relationships, and social contexts shape psychological wellbeing during this important stage of life. Drawing on developmental psychology and mental health research, students will examine topics such as identity development, friendships, romantic relationships, emotional wellbeing, resilience, stress management, social media use, and life purpose. The course also addresses common challenges faced by college students, including uncertainty about the future, academic and career pressures, loneliness, and mental health concerns. Through scientific research, real-life examples, self-reflection, and practical applications, students will develop a deeper understanding of themselves and others, learn evidence-based strategies for maintaining psychological wellbeing, and build skills that support personal growth and flourishing throughout emerging adulthood.','0','0','seed:build_edu_seed','2026-11-15 00:00:00','',NULL,''),
