(101915,'LIT2100','文学概论','','LIT - Literature','2100','L',3.00,2000,'Major','已评分','讲演','Main Campus','人文社科学院','人文社科学院','saved_outline','saved_terms','2024-25 Term 1
2025-26 Term 1
2026-27 Term 1','Successful completion of ENG1001 or equivalence recognized by HSS.','NIL','本课程将从小说、诗歌以及戏剧作品介绍世界英语文学。学生将学习基本的文学术语与概念，细读各种文学文本，分析作品的今昔重要性。在此过程中，学生将掌握文学分析与欣赏的技巧和语言技能。本课程鼓励学生通过阅读不同时期及不同文化背景的文学作品，拓宽视野，深化对人类经验的理解。本课程是英语文学副修项目的必修课。','This course introduces you to the world of literature in English, covering works of fiction, poetry and drama. You will learn basic literary terms and concepts, and closely study a variety of literary texts. We will also analyze the significance of each literary work for its time and for our time. In the process, you will acquire techniques and language skills for literary analysis and appreciation. You are encouraged to read literary works from different time periods and different cultural backgrounds to broaden and deepen your understanding of the human experience. This is a required course for the Minor in English Literature program.','0','0','seed:build_edu_seed','2026-11-15 00:00:00','',NULL,''),
(101916,'LIT3001','爱与战争文学','','LIT - Literature','3001','L',3.00,3000,'Major','已评分','讲演','Main Campus','人文社科学院','人文社科学院','saved_outline','saved_terms','2022-23 Term 2
2023-24 Term 1
2023-24 Term 2
2024-25 Term 1','Successful completion of at least one English foundation course (the ENL/ENG series or equivalence as recognized by HSS).','None.','本课程旨在进一步推动学生对文学的兴趣和互动性，其涵盖文学中两大主题—爱和战争。是什么让爱和战争如此有吸引力？是否是出于爱无所失、战无所得的“风险”？当爱和战争交汇，其危险性岂不直线上升？本课程将通过这些问题探究爱的本质、战争的原因以及人们如何分辨二者的风险，引导我们探索人们如何生活以及成功把控生命的危险和安逸。本课程旨在帮助学生更好地理解现代“所谓的西方”文学和文化，了解自己的同时，认识自我与所属文化和世界之间的关系，培养学生阅读力、批判性思维及研究能力，并提升英语的听说读写。','This is a fun course to advance your interest in and engagement with literature. It considers two of the greatest themes in literature: Love and War. And what makes these themes so compelling? Isn’t it the idea of risk – that there is much to be gained and much to be lost? And when love intersects with war, doesn’t that intersection increase the danger exponentially? So, this course is going to consider questions about the nature of love, the causes of war, and the way that people navigate the risks of both. It will allow us to ask questions about how people live and successfully manage life’s dangers (and pleasures). The course helps students to better understand contemporary “western” literature and culture. It also helps students to learn about themselves and their relationship to their culture and to the world around us. The course develops student reading, critical thinking, and research skills. It enhances students’ ability to read, write, hear (and understand) and speak in English.','0','0','seed:build_edu_seed','2026-11-15 00:00:00','',NULL,''),
(101917,'LIT3002','美国短篇小说','','LIT - Literature','3002','L',3.00,3000,'Major','已评分','讲演','Main Campus','人文社科学院','人文社科学院','saved_outline','saved_terms','2022-23 Term 2
2023-24 Term 2','Successful completion of at least one English foundation course (the ENL/ENG series or equivalence as recognized by HSS).','None','本课程旨在介绍以美国独特历史、社会文化发展为背景的短故事形式，探究何为短故事、写作方式以及其在美国发展的社会文化发展的推动力。课程将帮助学生更加理解现代美国文化以及其他西方文化对人们生活方式、信仰、性格的推动因素。学生将通过调查美国短故事的历史发展，思考其在美国历史下的主要主题。本课程将帮助学生认识自我、了解个人与其所属文化、世界的关系，并培养学生建立批判性思维，提升英语听说读写能力。','This course introduces students to the short story form, and in particular the American short story (with its unique history and social and cultural development). So what is different about a “short” story? How are they written? What social and cultural forces drove the development of the short story in America? In turn, what influence has the short story had over the development of American (and even global) culture? The course helps students to better understand contemporary American culture (and other “western” cultures) and the forces that shape peoples’ lifestyles, beliefs, and personalities. Students will survey the historical development of the American short story considering its major themes in the context of American history. The course also helps students to learn about themselves and their relationship to their culture and to the world. The course develops critical thinking and enhances students’ ability to read, write, hear (and understand) and speak in English.','0','0','seed:build_edu_seed','2026-11-15 00:00:00','',NULL,''),
(101918,'LIT3003','莎士比亚各时期全览','','LIT - Literature','3003','L',3.00,3000,'Major','已评分','讲演','Main Campus','人文社科学院','人文社科学院','saved_outline','saved_terms','2025-26 Term 2','Successful completion of ENG1001 or equivalence recognized by HSS','NIL','莎士比亚各时期全览（LIT 3003）是英语文学辅修课程的选修课。本课程旨在帮助学生全面了解莎士比亚的戏剧以及伊丽莎白一世和詹姆士一世时期英国的戏剧世界。它考察了莎士比亚戏剧的不同类型，并从中追溯莎士比亚作为剧作家的发展。它细读莎士比亚的四部戏剧，分别属于喜剧、历史、悲剧和浪漫类型。在阅读这些戏剧时，它重点解读戏剧文本来源、文化相关性、情节、人物和主题。','LIT3003 is an elective course of the English Literature Minor Programme. This course aims to help students gain a comprehensive understanding of Shakespeare’s plays and the dramatic world of Elizabethan and Jacobean England. It examines the different genres of the Shakespearean corpus and trace Shakespeare’s development as a playwright. It offers close studies of four of Shakespeare’s plays, one each from the genres of comedy, history, tragedy, and romance. In reading those plays, it covers the topics of sources, cultural relevance, critical reception, plots, characters, and themes.','0','0','seed:build_edu_seed','2026-11-15 00:00:00','',NULL,''),
(101919,'LIT3007','剧本创作介绍','','LIT - Literature','3007','L',3.00,3000,'Major','已评分','讲演','Main Campus','人文社科学院','人文社科学院','saved_outline','saved_terms','2023-24 Term 2
2024-25 Term 2','Successful completion of at least two English foundation courses (the ENL/ENG series or equivalence as recognized by HSS).','Nil.','本课程旨在为学生提供有关戏剧创意写作的入门指导，并让学生熟悉此领域的基本术语和策略。我们将从研究剧本写作形式开始，包括阅读关于技巧的论述和杰出剧作家的有效剧本示例，并以此为基础，进行课堂写作练习和作业。','This course is designed as an introductory playwriting workshop, meaning that it intends to give a broad introduction to creative practice in writing for theater as well as to acquaint the students with the basic terminologies and strategies of this form. We will begin the semester by looking at the form of playwriting—including reading essays on craft and examples of effective plays from recognized (and sometimes provocative) playwrights—and move on from there to in-class writing exercises and assignments.','0','0','seed:build_edu_seed','2026-11-15 00:00:00','',NULL,''),
(101920,'LIT3008','小说创作','','LIT - Literature','3008','L',3.00,3000,'Major','已评分','讲演','Main Campus','人文社科学院','人文社科学院','saved_outline','saved_terms','2025-26 Term 2','Successful completion of ENG1001 English Bridge Program (EBP) or equivalence approved by HSS.','','科目将通过学习诗歌、小说、纪实文章创作，引导学生了解什么是文学、如何创作、作品吸引性与乏味的区别、作家创作的动力、文化与文学之间的相互影响以及如何理解文学。本科目旨在助力学生写出创意性作品，同时帮助学生更好地理解现代西方文学文化以及理解塑造人们生活方式、信仰、性格的因素。科目内容会节选英语著作或作品的英语译本，帮助学生了解自己的同时，认识自我与所属文化和世界之间的关系，培养学生阅读力、批判性思维及研究能力，并提升英语听说读写能力。','This course introduces students to writing poetry, fiction, and creative non-fiction. It asks: What is literature? How is it written? What makes some writing interesting while other writing is boring? What forces drive writers to create? What are the cultural influences on literature and what influences does literature exert on culture? How is literature read and understood by readers? This course focuses exclusively on helping students who want (or think they might want) to write creative works. The course also helps students to better understand contemporary “western” cultures and the forces that shape peoples’ lifestyles, beliefs, and personalities. Students will read select literary works written in (or translated into) English. The course also helps students to learn about themselves and their relationship to their culture and to the world. The course develops critical thinking and enhances students’ ability to read, write, hear (and understand) and speak in English.','0','0','seed:build_edu_seed','2026-11-15 00:00:00','',NULL,''),
(101921,'LIT3009','短篇小说艺术','','LIT - Literature','3009','L',3.00,3000,'Major','已评分','讲演','Main Campus','人文社科学院','人文社科学院','saved_outline','saved_terms','2025-26 Term 2','Successful completion of ENG1001 English Bridge Program (EBP) or equivalence approved by HSS.','','短篇小说能有效帮助英语初学者过渡到长篇作品的学习。虽然不等同于小说，但二者之间的文学手法相通。通过学习经典著作，学生可了解短篇小说的基本要素，如背景、人物、情节和主题；与之相关著名的审美宣言也会纳入阅读书目中。每节课会围绕一个主题深度讨论，如“性别之间的冲突张力”、“显现和幻灭”。科目旨在培养学生提升其在短篇小说的阅读和写作能力，以极为透彻有效的视角理解生命深奥和人生百态的复杂性。','Short fiction is a significant literary genre that can effectively prepare English beginners for the study of longer works. Although a short story differs from a novel, authors of both draw from a common pool of literary techniques. In this course, students will be introduced to the fundamental elements of a short story, such as setting, character, plot, and theme, by closely studying exemplary works by masters. Additionally, famous aesthetic manifestos relevant to the genre of short fiction will be included in the reading list. Each lecture in this course will revolve around a central theme. Interesting themes such as “tension between the sexes,” and “epiphany and disillusionment” will be dealt with in some length. This course will help students better understand how short stories convey the profundity of life and the complexity of the human condition in the most lucid and effective manner possible.','0','0','seed:build_edu_seed','2026-11-15 00:00:00','',NULL,''),
